\begin{abstract}
Gaia astrometry has turned the tidal tails of the Hyades from a theoretical expectation into an observed structure. We review how the tails were first detected close to the cluster, how a model-based selection extended the candidate members to the full expected length of about 800\,pc, and why the asymmetry between the leading and the trailing tail has become a test of gravitational dynamics.
\end{abstract}
